#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""npu-transcribe - turn audio/video files into transcripts on the Intel NPU.

Russian is transcribed with GigaAM v3 (Sber), every other language with Whisper (OpenVINO
GenAI's WhisperPipeline), which also detects the language. All models run on the Intel NPU
(Core Ultra); the CPU is only the fallback when the NPU is missing or fails. The installer
runs it in its own venv through a wrapper in ~/.local/bin. Audio is decoded by the
ffmpeg CLI straight into memory (16 kHz mono float32, no temp files) and fed to the
model in blocks of at most 30 s (one Whisper window) cut at the quietest moment, so
memory stays flat for multi-hour recordings.

Outputs (next to the input file unless --out is given):
  NAME.srt  subtitles, one Whisper segment per cue
  NAME.md   readable paragraphs with [hh:mm:ss] markers about every 30 s
  NAME.txt  the same paragraphs without markers (only with --format ...,txt)
Existing files are never overwritten (your own NAME.md notes, downloaded NAME.srt
subtitles, an earlier transcript): the outputs then become NAME.2.*, NAME.3.*, ...
unless --overwrite is given.

With --speakers, the voices are told apart (NVIDIA's Nemotron 3 Diarization, also on the
NPU) and every line is labelled Speaker 1, Speaker 2, ... in the order they first speak.
--meeting DIR turns a BMeet meeting recording (DIR/part-N-mic.wav + part-N-remote.wav,
written by bmeet-meeting-audio) into a Markdown transcript: the microphone is "You",
the voices in the system audio become Person 1, Person 2, ... Your words from when the
browser call could not hear you (muted in Meet or Slack; BMeet's Chrome extension logs
it) are left out. An in-person meeting (microphone only) is split by voice: You, Person 1, ...
--live DIR writes the transcript of such a recording while it is still being made, to
DIR/live.md, for the BMeet window: You and Remote (Room in person), updated as people talk.

Examples:
  npu-transcribe meeting.m4a
  npu-transcribe --model small --format srt,md,txt --out ~/transcripts *.mp4
  npu-transcribe --language ru --translate interview.ogg   # Russian speech -> English text
  npu-transcribe --speakers interview.m4a                    # Speaker 1: ... Speaker 2: ...
  npu-transcribe --num-speakers 3 panel.mp4                  # exactly three voices
  npu-transcribe --engine whisper russian-talk.ogg           # Whisper even for Russian
  npu-transcribe --meeting DIR --output 2026-10-12_1041-meeting.md
  npu-transcribe --live DIR                                  # while DIR is being recorded

Notes:
  * Russian (given with --language ru, or detected) goes to GigaAM v3 when it is installed:
    ~20 % fewer word errors than Whisper turbo on Russian conversation and ~15x faster,
    but it mangles English words inside Russian speech. So the sentences where GigaAM
    wrote Latin letters also go to Whisper, and Whisper's English words replace GigaAM's
    garbled ones (--no-english-terms turns that off). --engine whisper keeps Whisper;
    --translate always uses Whisper (docs/npu-transcribe.md).
  * Whisper models: turbo (default when installed) is the more accurate one and on the
    NPU about as fast as small. small needs less RAM (~2.6 vs ~3.9 GB RSS), compiles faster and is
    ~3x faster on the CPU, so it is the better choice without the NPU.
  * The first NPU run compiles the model (small ~1 min, turbo ~4 min); the result
    is cached in ~/.cache/npu-ov-cache and later loads take a few seconds.
  * --translate always produces English. whisper-large-v3-turbo was not trained
    for translation, so --translate switches to the small model unless --model
    was given explicitly.
  * --language auto detects the language once per file (first non-silent block).
    On the NPU this needs a fresh pipeline per file (~3 s reload, see
    Whisper.ready_for_detection); pass --language to skip that in big batches.
  * Models live in ~/.local/share/npu-models (env NPU_MODELS_DIR); the speaker models in
    its speaker-diarization/ folder. Env NPU_DIARIZATION=pyannote tells the voices apart
    the way of before 0.6.0 (pyannote segmentation + clustered TitaNet fingerprints).
  * --speakers keeps the whole file in memory (~230 MB per hour of audio) and uses
    Whisper's word timestamps, so a sentence that crosses a change of speaker is split.
  * Exit status: 0 all files done, 1 some file failed, 2 usage error, 130 Ctrl+C.
  * Every finished file (done or failed) is appended as one JSON line to
    ~/.local/share/npu-transcribe/history.jsonl (env NPU_TRANSCRIBE_HISTORY),
    which the BMeet app and panel widget list.
"""
